\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

We evaluate generational depth estimation on nine model configurations spanning five architecture families:
Pythia-1.4B, OLMo-1B, Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct (rewrite and continuation), Qwen2.5-1.5B (base), Qwen2.5-7B, Qwen2.5-14B, LLaMA-3.1-8B, and Mistral-7B, all in continuation mode except where noted.
All experiments use the OBD-LightGBM classifier with the 15-dimensional feature set (Section~\ref{sec:method}), with 5{,}000 texts per depth level (generations 0--5; power analysis in Appendix~\ref{sec:power_curve}).

%% ====================================================================
\subsection{\texorpdfstring{$\Kempirical \leq 2$}{K*<=2} Across Model Configurations (C4)}
\label{sec:kstar_boundary}

\begin{table*}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Pairwise $\auc$ for adjacent depth pairs with bootstrap 95\% CIs and permutation $p$-values for boundary rows.
$\Kempirical = 2$ requires 1v2 CI lower bound $> \theta{=}0.60$ and 2v3 CI lower bound $\leq \theta$ (Eq.~\ref{eq:kstar}).
Extended results (all models) in Table~\ref{tab:kstar_ci_extended}.}
\label{tab:kstar_ci}
\begin{tabular}{lcccccccccc}
\toprule
 & \multicolumn{2}{c}{Qwen-rw} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{Pythia} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{OLMo} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{Qwen-cont} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{Qwen-7B\rlap{$^\ddagger$}} \\
\cmidrule(lr){2-3}\cmidrule(lr){4-5}\cmidrule(lr){6-7}\cmidrule(lr){8-9}\cmidrule(lr){10-11}
 & $\auc$ & 95\% CI & $\auc$ & 95\% CI & $\auc$ & 95\% CI & $\auc$ & 95\% CI & $\auc$ & 95\% CI \\
\midrule
0v1 & .9956 & & .9932 & & .9970 & & .9971 & & .9638 & [.961,\,.967] \\
1v2 & .6955 & [.686,\,.704]\rlap{$^*$} & .6539 & [.645,\,.663]\rlap{$^*$} & .6145 & [.605,\,.625]\rlap{$^*$} & .6112 & [.602,\,.621]\rlap{$^*$} & .6168 & [.608,\,.626]\rlap{$^*$} \\
2v3 & .5893 & [.580,\,.599] & .5514 & [.542,\,.563] & .5496 & [.540,\,.560] & .5609 & [.551,\,.571] & .5492 & [.539,\,.559] \\
3v4 & .5435 & & .5152 & & .5091 & & .5409 & & .5276 & \\
4v5 & .5340 & & .5136 & & .5045 & & .5141 & & .5085 & \\
\bottomrule
\multicolumn{11}{l}{\footnotesize $^*$Permutation $p < 0.001$ for all 1v2 comparisons shown.} \\
\multicolumn{11}{l}{\footnotesize $^\ddagger$Generator: Qwen2.5-7B (fp16); scorer: Qwen2.5-1.5B.}
\end{tabular}
\end{table*}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig2_auc_decay.pdf}
\caption{Pairwise $\auc$ decay across depth pairs for model-mode configurations (solid) and decoding strategies (dashed). The horizontal dashed line marks $\theta = 0.60$.}
\label{fig:auc_decay}
\end{figure}

Table~\ref{tab:kstar_ci} and Figure~\ref{fig:auc_decay} show $\Kempirical \leq 2$ across all 9 model configurations: 8/9 yield $\Kempirical{=}2$ (1v2 $\auc$ range: 0.6112--0.6955; all CI lower bounds $> \theta$; permutation $p < 0.001$), while Mistral-7B is the only exception ($\Kempirical{=}1$; 1v2 $\auc = 0.594$, below $\theta$; Section~\ref{sec:boundary_cases}).
LLaMA-3.1-8B yields $\Kempirical{=}2$ (0v1 $\auc = 0.9225$, 1v2 $\auc = 0.6123$, 95\% CI $[0.603, 0.622]$; borderline: 1/5 folds falls below $\theta$; Table~\ref{tab:kstar_ci_extended}).
All four decoding strategies likewise yield $\Kempirical{=}2$ (Table~\ref{tab:s2_decoding}).
The 1v2 spread due to generation mode ($\Delta = 8.4$\,pp within Qwen) exceeds the spread across models in continuation mode ($\Delta = 3.9$\,pp).

\paragraph{Scale and instruction-tuning invariance.}
$\Kempirical{=}2$ persists from 1.5B through 14B: Qwen-14B (1v2 $\auc = 0.621$ $[0.612, 0.630]$), Qwen-7B ($0.6168$ $[0.608, 0.626]$), and LLaMA-3.1-8B ($0.6123$) all exceed $\theta$, despite progressively reduced 0v1 signal (0.960 at 14B, 0.964 at 7B, 0.923 at 8B vs.\ $> 0.99$ at $\leq$1.5B).
A base-vs.-instruct ablation at 1.5B likewise yields $\Kempirical = 2$ (1v2 $\auc = 0.626$); the boundary is not an instruction-tuning artifact.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_kstar_theta.pdf}
\caption{$\Kempirical(\theta)$ sensitivity curve for six configurations. $\Kempirical$: maximum discriminable depth; $\theta$: AUC decision threshold. All configurations yield $\Kempirical{=}2$ at $\theta{=}0.60$ (dashed line). At $\theta{=}0.70$, all drop to $\Kempirical{=}1$.}
\label{fig:kstar_theta}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:kstar_theta} shows $\Kempirical$ as a function of $\theta$: web text $\Kempirical{=}2$ is stable across a 5\,pp window ($\theta \in [0.575, 0.625]$), with a gradual transition to $\Kempirical{=}3$ at $\theta{=}0.55$ (two Qwen configurations) and $\Kempirical{=}1$ at $\theta{=}0.70$.
The domain ordering (arXiv $>$ web $\geq$ cross-scorer) is preserved across the full $\theta$ range (Table~\ref{tab:theta_sensitivity}; Figure~\ref{fig:theta_sensitivity} in Appendix shows the complete model $\times$ domain $\theta$ sensitivity).
All four decoding strategies likewise yield $\Kempirical = 2$ at $\theta{=}0.60$ (1v2 $\auc \in [0.687, 0.704]$; Table~\ref{tab:s2_decoding}).

%% ====================================================================
\subsection{Largely Generator-Independent Transfer}
\label{sec:transfer}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.85\columnwidth]{figures/fig3_transfer_heatmap.pdf}
\caption{Cross-model transfer heatmap. Each cell shows 6-class ordinal accuracy when trained on the row model and tested on the column model, divided by in-domain accuracy (retention rate). Pythia$\leftrightarrow$OLMo: 90--92\%; Qwen-rewrite$\to$continuation: 61--63\%.}
\label{fig:transfer_heatmap}
\end{figure}

Continuation-mode classifiers transfer well across generators (Figure~\ref{fig:transfer_heatmap}; full matrix in Table~\ref{tab:s1_transfer}): Pythia$\leftrightarrow$OLMo retains 90--92\%, Qwen-cont$\leftrightarrow$Pythia 84--85\%, Qwen-cont$\leftrightarrow$OLMo 80--85\% (0v1 cross-model $\auc > 0.96$ for all pairs).
Qwen-rewrite classifiers transfer poorly (61--63\%), consistent with reliance on mode-specific artifacts rather than generalizable depth features.

\paragraph{Cross-scorer validation.}
Replacing the self-scorer with Pythia-1.4B or OLMo-1B preserves feature rankings ($\rho > 0.90$; Table~\ref{tab:crossscorer}).
$\Kempirical$ is largely robust to scorer choice: across 22 cross-scorer configurations on C4 and arXiv, $\Kempirical$ was preserved in 20 cases; the two exceptions (Qwen-cont scored by Pythia on C4, Qwen-7B cross-scored on arXiv) are borderline, with AUC within 0.002 of $\theta$.
Qwen-cont is the most scorer-sensitive continuation-mode configuration: its self-scored 1v2 CI lower bound exceeds $\theta$ by only 0.2\,pp (Table~\ref{tab:kstar_ci}), and cross-family scoring pushes it below $\theta$.
For arXiv, cross-scoring with Pythia-1.4B preserves $\Kempirical{=}3$ for three of four generators (Qwen-1.5B, LLaMA-8B, Mistral-7B; 2v3 $\auc$ range: 0.608--0.632).
Qwen-7B exhibits a borderline decrease to $\Kempirical{=}2$ (2v3 $\auc = 0.599$ vs.\ $0.626$ self-scored, $\Delta = -2.7$\,pp; 3/5 folds above $\theta$), consistent with the Qwen family's scorer sensitivity observed on C4 and Wikipedia.
Bootstrap CIs (10{,}000 resamples) confirm $\Kempirical{=}3$ for Qwen-1.5B (CI lower $= 0.617$) and LLaMA-8B (CI lower $= 0.605$); for Mistral-7B (CI: $[0.593, 0.622]$) and Qwen-7B (CI: $[0.584, 0.613]$), the CI straddles $\theta$; their $\Kempirical{=}3$ assignment is sensitive to resampling variance (Table~\ref{tab:arxiv_crossscorer_ci}).
A three-way ANOVA (Generator $\times$ Scorer $\times$ Depth-Pair; $N{=}300$; Table~\ref{tab:anova_crossscorer}) confirms that Depth-Pair dominates variance ($\eta^2_p = 0.998$); Scorer is statistically significant ($p < .001$) but practically minor ($\eta^2_p = 0.192$), consistent with a detectable but small scorer influence on absolute discriminability.

%% ====================================================================
\subsection{Feature Ordering and Generation Mode}
\label{sec:feature_degradation}
\label{sec:generation_mode}

Among continuation-mode models, single-feature $\auc$ rankings are highly correlated ($\rho \geq 0.85$; Table~\ref{tab:top_features}).
The top-3 features (\texttt{surp\_d1\_std}, \texttt{surp\_std}, \texttt{surp\_d2\_std}) are shared across all three families: autoregressive generation progressively damps surprisal variability; however, no single feature subset of size ${<}8$ reliably sustains $\Kempirical{=}2$ (Appendix~\ref{sec:feature_subset}).
A feature group ablation (Appendix~\ref{sec:feature_ablation}) confirms that surprisal (base) dominates on arXiv (single AUC $= 0.809$, LOO $\Delta = 0.031$); on C4 and Wikipedia, all LOO drops fall within fold variance ($\leq 0.007$).
Qwen-rewrite diverges (\texttt{freq\_entropy} dominates), consistent with its poor cross-model transfer (61--63\%).
Within Qwen, rewrite yields 1v2 $\auc = 0.6955$ vs.\ continuation's $0.6112$ ($\Delta = +8.4$\,pp); continuation mode is therefore recommended as the canonical condition.

%% ====================================================================
\subsection{Neural Probe}
\label{sec:neural_probe}

Replacing the 15D features with frozen encoder hidden states (MLP probe, 5{,}000 texts per depth; Table~\ref{tab:neural_probe_crossmodel}), Qwen achieves $\Kempirical^{\text{neural}} = 3$ (2v3 $\auc = 0.6159$), while Pythia and OLMo remain at $\Kempirical^{\text{neural}} = 2$ ($\auc_{2v3}$: 0.5963, 0.5892).
The Qwen-only elevation reflects self-familiarity artifacts that transfer poorly (Section~\ref{sec:transfer}); the 15D features yield consistent $\Kempirical = 2$ across all families.

\noindent\begin{minipage}{\columnwidth}
\centering
\footnotesize
\captionof{table}{Neural probe pairwise AUC using frozen-encoder MLP. Each cell reports AUC for classifying depth $d$ vs.\ $d{+}1$ text. $\Kempirical^{\text{neural}}$: maximum depth where AUC exceeds $\theta = 0.60$.}
\label{tab:neural_probe_crossmodel}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{2v3 $\auc$} & \textbf{95\% CI} & $\Kempirical^{\text{neural}}$ \\
\midrule
Qwen-1.5B  & 0.6159 & $[0.610,\,0.622]$ & 3 \\
Pythia-1.4B & 0.5963 & $[0.589,\,0.603]$ & 2 \\
OLMo-1B    & 0.5892 & $[0.582,\,0.597]$ & 2 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{minipage}

%% ====================================================================
\subsection{Cross-Domain Variation}
\label{sec:cross_domain}

The preceding experiments use C4 (general web text).
To test whether $\Kempirical$ reflects domain structure or model identity, we evaluate on arXiv abstracts ($n{=}2{,}200$ per model) and Wikipedia ($n{=}5{,}000$ per model) across six model configurations spanning four architecture families (Table~\ref{tab:domain_kstar}).

\paragraph{arXiv ($\Kempirical{=}3$): five-model cross-validation.}
All five domain-competent models yield $\Kempirical{=}3$ on arXiv, with boundary-pair $\auc$ (2v3) ranging from 0.6215 to 0.6545, all exceeding $\theta$.
The elevated signal reflects higher per-generation distributional shift (1v2 JSD $= 0.077$ vs.\ ${\sim}0.01$ for C4): formulaic features (LaTeX formatting, citation patterns, mathematical notation) degrade slowly under autoregressive generation and supply persistent depth signal across four architecture families and 1.4B--8B parameters.
Removing LaTeX markup reduces 2v3 AUC by ${\sim}$1.8\,pp but preserves $\Kempirical{=}3$ (CI lower $= 0.610 > \theta$; Table~\ref{tab:latex_ablation}); content-level properties dominate.
OLMo-1B constitutes a capacity failure rather than a counter-example; its arXiv generations are entirely out-of-domain (0v1 JSD $= 0.33$, near-total information loss at the first step), yielding $\Kempirical{=}1$.
Mistral-7B provides the strongest domain-dependence evidence: $\Kempirical{=}1$ on C4 (Section~\ref{sec:boundary_cases}) but $\Kempirical{=}3$ on arXiv: domain structure alone can shift $\Kempirical$ by two levels within the same model.

\paragraph{Wikipedia ($\Kempirical{=}1$): single-generation saturation.}
With scorer confound controlled (Pythia-1.4B for Qwen generators; Qwen-1.5B, itself cross-family, for all others), all six models yield $\Kempirical{=}1$ on Wikipedia: 1v2 $\auc$ ranges from 0.499 (OLMo) to 0.595 (LLaMA), all below $\theta$.
Wikipedia represents the simplest domain tested; its encyclopedic regularity provides minimal structural signal beyond the 0v1 boundary, and a single generation pass suffices for complete saturation.
A same-family scorer confound inflates the apparent $\Kempirical$ for Qwen generators: Qwen-1.5B scoring yields $\Kempirical{=}2$ (1v2 $\auc \approx 0.61$--$0.62$), but cross-family scoring reduces 1v2 by 2.4\,pp to below $\theta$ (Appendix~\ref{sec:wiki_domain}).
This +1 depth bias under same-family scoring is specific to Qwen generators on Wikipedia and does not affect C4 results (Section~\ref{sec:transfer}).

\paragraph{Code ($\Kempirical{=}1$--$2$): cross-modality confirmation.}
Python functions from CodeSearchNet~\cite{husain2019codesearchnet} ($n{=}5{,}000$) yield $\Kempirical{=}2$ for both Qwen2.5-1.5B (1v2 $\auc = 0.738$; Table~\ref{tab:code_domain_qwen}) and LLaMA-3.1-8B ($\auc = 0.739$), but borderline $\Kempirical{=}1$ for Pythia-1.4B ($\auc = 0.598$, CI upper $= 0.603$; Table~\ref{tab:code_crossmodel_kstar}).
Code matches C4 despite a different modality, with the same sharp 2v3 collapse (JSD ratio $11.7$--$13.5\times$).
Cross-family scorer validation confirms Code $\Kempirical{=}2$ is text-intrinsic ($\Delta \leq 1.9$\,pp; Appendix~\ref{sec:code_domain}).

\paragraph{Domain as the primary factor.}
With the scorer confound controlled, the domain ordering is: arXiv ($\Kempirical{=}3$) $>$ C4 ($\Kempirical{=}2$) $\geq$ Code ($\Kempirical{=}1$--$2$) $\geq$ Wikipedia ($\Kempirical{=}1$).
Scale (1B--14B) and architecture do not alter the C4/arXiv/Wikipedia ordering; code shows model-dependent variation at the boundary, but domain structural complexity remains the primary determinant of $\Kempirical$.

\noindent\begin{minipage}{\columnwidth}
\centering
\footnotesize
\captionof{table}{Domain $\times$ Model $\Kempirical$ matrix (scorer-controlled). Wikipedia values use cross-family scorer (Pythia-1.4B). $^*$OLMo arXiv: capacity failure. $^\dagger$Same-family scorer yields $\Kempirical{=}2$; controlled value shown. $^\ddagger$Borderline: 1v2 $\auc = 0.598$, CI upper $= 0.603 > \theta$.}
\label{tab:domain_kstar}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{C4} & \textbf{Code} & \textbf{arXiv} & \textbf{Wikipedia} \\
\midrule
Qwen-1.5B    & 2 & 2 & 3 & 1$^\dagger$ \\
Qwen-7B      & 2 & --- & 3 & 1$^\dagger$ \\
Pythia-1.4B  & 2 & 1$^\ddagger$ & 3 & 1 \\
OLMo-1B      & 2 & --- & 1$^*$ & 1 \\
LLaMA-3.1-8B & 2 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\
Mistral-7B   & 1 & --- & 3 & 1 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{minipage}

%% ====================================================================
\subsection{Binary Detector Confidence}
\label{sec:binary_confidence}

Binary detectors (DivEye~\cite{basani2026diveye}, Binoculars~\cite{hans2024binoculars}) show moderate rank correlations with depth ($|\rho| = 0.06$--$0.46$; Table~\ref{tab:s9_binary_spearman}) but fail as depth proxies: 6-class accuracy is at or below chance ($\leq 0.199$ vs.\ 0.167 baseline), pairwise $\auc$ yields $\Kempirical^{\text{binary}} \leq 1$, and multiple conditions exhibit non-monotonic AUC at deeper pairs.
The observed correlation is an artifact of 0v1 separation rather than fine-grained depth signal.

